{"name":"Camel Toe","hex":"#ac8a2a","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"camel toe","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Knurled fingers.","Alternative form of cameltoe."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/camel_toe"}],"entries":[{"title":"Camel toe","blocks":[{"text":"Camel toe, or cameltoe, is slang for the outline of the labia majora in tightly fitting clothes. Owing to a combination of anatomical factors and the fabric tension in the crotch area, the outer labia and mons pubis may, together, display a shape resembling the forefoot of a camel. Camel toe commonly occurs as a result of wearing clothing with crotch-area vertical tension, such as Spandex activewear or athleisure bottom wear, hotpants or swimwear."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Miss_Desiree_cameltoe.jpg/330px-Miss_Desiree_cameltoe.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camel_toe","wikidata":"Q1028513"},{"title":"Camel","blocks":[{"text":"A camel is an even-toed ungulate in the genus Camelus that bears distinctive fatty deposits known as \"humps\" on its back. Camels have long been domesticated and, as livestock, they provide food and textiles. Camels are working animals especially suited to their desert habitat and are a vital means of transport for passengers and cargo. There are three surviving species of camel. The one-humped dromedary makes up 94% of the world's camel population, and the two-humped Bactrian camel makes up 6%. The wild Bactrian camel is a distinct species that is not ancestral to the domestic Bactrian camel, and is now endangered, with around 1,000 individuals and a continuing decline."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/07._Camel_Profile%2C_near_Silverton%2C_NSW%2C_07.07.2007.jpg/330px-07._Camel_Profile%2C_near_Silverton%2C_NSW%2C_07.07.2007.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camel","wikidata":"Q7375"},{"title":"Toe","blocks":[{"text":"Toes are the digits of the foot of a tetrapod. Animal species such as cats that walk on their toes are described as being digitigrade. Humans, and other animals that walk on the soles of their feet, are described as being plantigrade; unguligrade animals are those that walk on hooves at the tips of their toes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Toes.png/330px-Toes.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toe","wikidata":"Q154425"},{"title":"Camel (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Camel is a color that resembles the color of the hair of a camel."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Camelcalf-feeding.jpg/330px-Camelcalf-feeding.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camel_(color)","wikidata":"Q17082553"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/21/Camel_Foot.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/21/Camel_Foot.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACamel_Foot.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Camels_by_country_per_capita.png/500px-Camels_by_country_per_capita.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Camels_by_country_per_capita.png/1280px-Camels_by_country_per_capita.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Camels_by_country_per_capita.png/500px-Camels_by_country_per_capita.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.